    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Mongol realm that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> allotted to his son Chagatai, spanning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>'s steppe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a> cities.</li><li>It produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a> and, through him, a Chagataid line that helped legitimise the Mughal emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>.</li><li>Torn between nomadic tribes and settled Muslim cities, it split apart, losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a> by 1370.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qarshi">Qarshi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> takes its name from the palace that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kebek-khan">Kebek Khan</a> built there, near Nasaf, in the early 14th century.</p></aside>
    </section>

