    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chagatai Khanate was not so much founded as allotted — a territorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appanage">appanage</a> (ulus) assigned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> to his second son Chagatai as part of the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>'s vast conquests, a status of inherited subordination that would shape the khanate's character for much of its existence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a> (r. 1227–1242) received lands formerly belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> and the Khwarazmian realm: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> (much of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ili-river">Ili River</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kashgaria">Kashgaria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#semirechye">Semirechye</a> steppe — a domain that included both the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a> cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bukhara">Bukhara</a> and the broad pastoral grasslands of the Central Asian steppe. This dual inheritance — urban commercial civilisation and nomadic pastoral tradition — was the khanate's defining structural tension from the start. Chagatai himself was a strict guardian of Mongol tradition: he enforced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yasa">Yasa</a> (Mongol customary law) rigorously and was remembered by Muslim chroniclers as hostile to Islamic practice, reportedly forbidding the ritual slaughter of animals and ablution in running water in accordance with Mongol custom. The khanate's subordination to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-khan">Great Khan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karakorum">Karakorum</a> was pronounced during Chagatai's lifetime: the sedentary cities of Transoxiana were administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahmud-yalavach">Mahmud Yalavach</a> and later his son Mas'ud Beg, Khwarazmian Muslims answerable to the Great Khan rather than to Chagatai, and the khanate had no independent foreign policy, little revenue base beyond the nomadic pastures, and limited capacity to act without the Great Khan's sanction. Its history for its first three decades was therefore less an independent political story than the chronicle of a subordinate domain within the Mongol imperial system, whose ruler's primary task was to keep order among the nomads of his pastures and provide contingents for the Great Khan's campaigns, until Alghu (r. 1260–1266) exploited the civil war between Kublai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> to assert real independence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#samarkand"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samarkand.jpg" alt="Samarkand" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#samarkand">Samarkand</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Registan_-_Gusjer.jpg: Gustavo Jeronimo from Aranjuez, Spain derivative work: MrPanyGoff, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chagatai Khanate's economy rested on one of the most important commercial networks of the pre-modern world: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a>, the web of overland routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> that passed through the khanate's Central Asian heartland. The cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> — <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bukhara">Bukhara</a> and the smaller commercial centres along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zerafshan">Zerafshan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oxus">Amu Darya</a> rivers — had been among the most prosperous urban centres in the world for centuries before the Mongol conquest, and despite the devastation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongol-invasion-of-central-asia">Mongol invasion of Central Asia</a> in 1219–1221 they recovered over time under Mongol protection of long-distance trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pax-mongolica">Pax Mongolica</a> of the late thirteenth and early fourteenth centuries, when Mongol guarantees of safe conduct allowed merchants to travel across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a> on an unprecedented scale, benefited the khanate through tolls and taxes on goods passing through its territory, although its frequent wars with the Yuan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> repeatedly disrupted the routes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kebek-khan">Kebek Khan</a>'s monetary reform of around 1321 — introducing a standardised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> coinage so widely trusted that the coins became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kebek-coin">kebeks</a> — was the most significant Chagatai contribution to the commercial infrastructure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The khanate's persistent economic problem was the tension between its nomadic aristocracy — whose wealth came from herds, war booty and tribute from subject sedentary populations — and the urban merchant class, which required stable government, predictable taxation and secure property. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarmashirin">Tarmashirin</a>'s shift of the political centre towards Transoxiana in the early 1330s was an attempt to resolve this tension in favour of the sedentary economy, but it alienated the eastern nomadic tribes whose military power remained the ultimate foundation of Chagataid authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a>'s rise to power in Transoxiana in the 1360s, completed by 1370, transferred the great commercial cities to a more effectively sedentary regime, ending the Chagataid hold on its richest inheritance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#kaidu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaidu.jpg" alt="Kaidu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#kaidu">Kaidu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qaidu_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maître de la Mazarine, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chagatai military was built on the Mongol tradition of mounted archery — unmatched mobility, the firepower of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#composite-bow">composite bow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#feigned-retreat">feigned retreats</a> and calculated terror — that had allowed the original Mongol armies to conquer peoples from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a>. The khanate exercised this tradition most effectively during the dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kaidu">Kaidu</a> (d. 1301), when the combined forces of Kaidu's Ögedeid domains and the Chagatai khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#duwa">Duwa</a> (r. 1282–1307), whom Kaidu had installed, waged sustained war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> and his successors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>, raiding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uyghurs">Uyghur</a> lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongolia">Mongolia</a> and keeping pressure on the Yuan frontier for nearly three decades. Chagataid armies also crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> to invade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> repeatedly from the 1290s, under commanders sent by Duwa such as his son Qutlugh Khwaja, who led a great army to the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> in 1299; but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>'s formidable forces turned back these invasions and prevented any permanent Mongol occupation of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>. A raid into India under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarmashirin">Tarmashirin</a> in the late 1320s, reportedly reaching deep into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#punjab">Punjab</a> towards Delhi, was a late spectacular projection of force — but it was a plundering expedition rather than a conquest and left the khanate's strategic position unchanged. The khanate's fundamental military weakness was its limited capacity for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-warfare">siege warfare</a>: Mongol cavalry was devastating in open country but struggled against strongly fortified cities of the kind that defended both Yuan and Indian territories. As the khanate fragmented after 1334, military power was dispersed among competing tribal confederations, each capable of raiding but none capable of coordinated strategic action on the scale the khanate had briefly achieved.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#timur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timur.jpg" alt="Timur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Timur_reconstruction03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">user:shakko, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chagatai Khanate's government was perpetually contested between the Genghisid legitimacy of the Chagataid khans and the practical power of the great tribal groupings — among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dughlat">Dughlat</a> in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#barlas">Barlas</a>, Jalayir and Qara'una in the west — whose military strength made them the khanate's effective rulers whenever a khan lacked the personal authority to enforce his will. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghisid-principle">Genghisid principle</a> — that only male-line descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> could hold the title of khan — was both the dynasty's greatest asset and a severe constraint: the Chagataid line could not be replaced even by more effective rulers, producing the remarkable spectacle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a> (Tamerlane), the most powerful ruler of the late fourteenth-century Islamic world, governing through puppet Chagataid khans such as Soyurghatmish rather than taking the title himself, and styling himself güregen, “royal son-in-law”, through his marriage to a Genghisid princess. The khanate's legal framework drew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yasa">Yasa</a>, the Mongol customary law attributed to Genghis Khan, but the progressive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islamisation">Islamisation</a> of the western khanate brought in elements of Islamic law that conflicted with the Yasa's provisions, generating one of the central political tensions of the fourteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kebek-khan">Kebek Khan</a>'s (r. c. 1318–1326) effort to establish a settled seat of government near Nasaf — the palace that gave the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qarshi">Qarshi</a> its name — combined with his administrative division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> into districts and his currency reform, was the most serious attempt to turn the khanate from a nomadic confederation into a territorial state with consistent administrative capacity, but his successors lacked the will or the means to continue it. The fundamental failure was the inability to reconcile the nomadic eastern tribes, whose pastoral traditions and largely shamanist and Buddhist religious practices were threatened by Islamisation and sedentarisation, with the urban commercial class of Transoxiana, whose prosperity required exactly the stable, Islam-friendly government that the eastern tribes rejected.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ulugh-beg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg.jpg" alt="Ulugh Beg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulugh_Beg,_Timurid_painting_1425-50.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artistUnknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chagatai Khanate's historical impact was largely indirect, operating through the successor states it made possible rather than through its own institutional achievements. Its most consequential contribution was unintended: the political and military world of the Chagataid domains produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a> (Tamerlane, 1336–1405), a member of the Turkicised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#barlas">Barlas</a> tribe, who built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, the most powerful state of the late fourteenth- and fifteenth-century Islamic world, whose capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samarkand">Samarkand</a> became under Timur and his successors — notably the astronomer-prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> — one of the great centres of Islamic art, architecture and scholarship. The Chagataid dynastic line, used by Timur as a legitimising figurehead, continued to underpin political legitimacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a> long after the khanate's effective dissolution: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babur">Babur</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> (1526), was a Timurid on his father's side and a descendant of the Chagataid khans through his mother, making the Chagatai inheritance one of the most consequential genealogical threads in Asian imperial history. The khanate also gave its name to Chagatai Turkic, the literary language of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ali-shir-navai">Ali-Shir Nava'i</a> wrote his poetry and Babur his memoirs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a> commerce that the khanate taxed during its period of effective control formed part of the transcontinental exchange of goods, technologies and ideas characteristic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pax-mongolica">Pax Mongolica</a> era — and of the routes along which, it is widely believed, the plague that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-death">Black Death</a> spread westward. The khanate's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islamisation">Islamisation</a>, culminating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarmashirin">Tarmashirin</a>'s conversion and confirmed in the east by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tughlugh-timur">Tughlugh Timur</a>'s conversion around 1354, helped fix the religious character of Central Asia as a Muslim region, with profound and long-lasting consequences from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#moghulistan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moghulistan.jpg" alt="Moghulistan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#moghulistan">Moghulistan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chagatai_Khanate_(1490).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ro4444 at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chagatai Khanate's decline was unusually slow and structurally complex, unfolding over nearly two centuries through the progressive loss of governing capacity rather than through foreign conquest or sudden collapse. The khanate never achieved the institutional consolidation needed to survive the loss of any particular khan: its government depended on the personal authority of the ruler and the temporary alignment of tribal interests, producing a rapid turnover of khans that reflected both the violent politics of the nomadic confederation and the inability of any individual ruler to create institutions that would outlast him. The terminal fracture came along the religious fault line that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarmashirin">Tarmashirin</a>'s conversion exposed: his promotion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> and his shift of the political centre to the sedentary cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> alienated the eastern nomadic tribes to the point of revolt in 1334, and his overthrow and death opened a period in which the western khanate fell under the control of tribal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amir">amirs</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qazaghan">Qazaghan</a>, who killed the khan Qazan in 1346–1347 and ruled through puppet khans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tughlugh-timur">Tughlugh Timur</a>, raised to power in the east by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dughlat">Dughlat</a>, briefly reunited the khanate in 1360–1361, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a>'s seizure of Transoxiana in the following decade removed the khanate's economically vital western half for good, leaving only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> — the eastern rump based on the Ili valley steppe and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> — as a surviving Chagataid state. Moghulistan endured in this diminished form for more than a century, its khans often dominated by the Dughlat amirs, enduring Timurid invasions from the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oirats">Oirat</a> raids from the north-east, before splitting in the late fifteenth century between rival khans and giving way to a separate eastern khanate based at Turpan and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> founded in 1514. The Chagataid line itself survived in these successors until their absorption by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dzungars">Dzungars</a> in the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries — but as an independent governing entity, the khanate that had once commanded the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oxus">Amu Darya</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#altai-mountains">Altai</a> had dissolved irreversibly into its nomadic and sedentary constituent parts.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Chagatai Khanate's most revealing characteristic was its chronic inability to decide whether it was a nomadic confederation or a sedentary state — a tension between steppe pastoral tradition and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a> urban civilisation it governed that produced the religious and cultural fault lines along which it repeatedly fractured, making it a paradigmatic example of how Mongol successor states were reshaped and ultimately consumed by the very societies they had conquered.</em></p>

